Think of a case study like a magazine feature, it cannot drag or get boring to rad.
Keep a case study digestible, thorough and a story.
Focus on insights rather than process.
Show your intention and your values.
Make your case study scannable with headlines, short paragraphs and captions.
Provide context to the project and your design process. Think about what your reader needs to know to appreciate this project. As concisely as possible, explain how you approached the problem and how you worked through it.
1. Title of project- Consider including the type of work in the title so we can easily see your specialities at a glance. 
2. Introduction & goal- Set the scene for your reader with a quick sentence explaining what this project is all about. What’s the product? What was the briefing? What challenge did you set out to solve? Did you have a certain idea or expectation for the project when you began?
3. Design process: The next section gives a glimpse of the design process we followed for solving the above problem. This helps the reader understand how we approached the problem statement. Did we do user research? Did we work on just visuals? Did we work on the prototyping tool? This is the section to tell about that. 
4. Process & experience- In a paragraph or two, explain how you worked through the project. Why did you choose that approach? Did you take a unique angle or notice some surprising insight? 
Ask yourself “why” a thousand times, and then answer those questions.
5. The outcome- What happened? Did you feel proud of the result? Did it increase the client’s sales by 2000%? A case study should ideally be a success story. If it's not, tell us why it's meaningful and what you learned from it.
6. Images with captions- If someone scrolls through your case study and only reads the headlines and captions, they should still understand your project. 
7. All users of the solution identified should be labelled as "persona #1" "persona #2" and so on. Give each persona a title name if possible.
8. Name sections to tell a story instead of as labels. For example: "user testing" section can be named "what our users said". "design process" can be "how we got here". The case study should flow like a story. 
